From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leszek Swirski <leszeks@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 14:17:37 +0200
Subject: [maglev] Only treat PREVIOUS_INDEX loads from InitialValue as
 non-aliasing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

KnownNodeAspects caches context slot values in `loaded_context_slots_`
keyed by `(ValueNode* context, int offset)`. When
`may_have_aliasing_contexts_ != kAlways`, a store to `(ctx_1, offset)`
only updates `(ctx_1, offset)` without invalidating `(ctx_2, offset)`
for `ctx_2 != ctx_1`. This is only sound if two distinct `ValueNode*`
pointers cannot point to the same runtime Context object.

Previously, UpdateMayHaveAliasingContexts walked chains of
`LoadContextSlotNoCells(PREVIOUS_INDEX)` to their root `context` before
switching on `context->opcode()`, assuming that a `PREVIOUS_INDEX` walk
shares the aliasing properties of its root. However, this is only valid
when the root is `InitialValue(<context>)` (the single incoming context
of the top-level compilation unit, whose `PREVIOUS_INDEX` walks are
deduplicated in `loaded_context_constants_`). For any other root — such
as an allocated context (`CreateFunctionContext` / `InlinedAllocation`)
or a `HeapConstant` — the parent context can already be represented in
the graph by a separate direct `ValueNode*`, causing two distinct
`ValueNode*` pointers (`A` and `A'`) to alias the same runtime Context.

For example, consider `outer` (context `A`) creating a context with `x`,
`mid` (context `B`, with `B.previous = A`) creating a large context and
returning a small closure `f` that writes `x = 2`, and `outer` calling
`f()`:

  function outer() {
    let x = 1;                  // lives in context A
    function mid() {
      let a0 = 0, ...;          // lives in context B (B.previous == A)
      return function f() {
        x = 2;                  // writes A[x] via B.previous
      };
    }
    let g = mid();
    x = 1;
    g();
    return x;
  }

1. During graph building of `outer`, context `A` is allocated:

     A: InlinedAllocation(ctx_outer)

2. In Turbolev, `MaglevInliner` pops call sites in priority order
   (small functions before regular ones) and does not run
   `MaglevGraphOptimizer` between inlining steps. When `f` (small) is
   inlined *before* `mid` (large), `g` is still the call result of
   `mid()`, so `f`'s context is an opaque load `B'` (aliasing `B`), and
   `MinimizeContextChainDepth` cannot fold `f`'s `PREVIOUS_INDEX` hop
   `A'` (aliasing `A`):

     B': LoadTaggedField(g, JSFunction::kContextOffset)
           |
           v  LoadContextSlotNoCells(PREVIOUS_INDEX)
     A': LoadContextSlotNoCells(B', PREVIOUS_INDEX)
           |
           v
         StoreContextSlotNoCells(A', slot_x, 2)

3. Next, `mid` is inlined. `mid` allocates context `B` with parent `A`
   and returns `FastCreateClosure(f, context = B)`:

     A:  InlinedAllocation(ctx_outer)
           ^
           |  stored at PREVIOUS_INDEX
     B:  InlinedAllocation(ctx_mid)

4. After inlining finishes, `MaglevGraphOptimizer` runs alongside
   `RecomputeKnownNodeAspectsProcessor`. It folds `B'` (loading
   `f.context_`) to `B`, and updates `A'`'s input in place from `B'` to
   `B`:

     A:  InlinedAllocation(ctx_outer)       <-- outer stores x=1, loads x
           ^
           |  previous
     B:  InlinedAllocation(ctx_mid)
           |
           v  LoadContextSlotNoCells(PREVIOUS_INDEX)
     A': LoadContextSlotNoCells(B, PREVIOUS_INDEX) <-- f stores x=2

   Now `A` and `A'` are two distinct `ValueNode*` pointers that both
   refer to `ctx_outer` at runtime:

     loaded_context_slots_ before g():
       (A,  slot_x) -> SmiConstant(1)

     StoreContextSlotNoCells(A', slot_x, 2) inside g():
       UpdateMayHaveAliasingContexts(A') walks A' -> B
       (InlinedAllocation), sees kInlinedAllocation, and leaves
       may_have_aliasing_contexts_ == kNever!

     loaded_context_slots_ after g():
       (A,  slot_x) -> SmiConstant(1)   // STALE! Not invalidated!
       (A', slot_x) -> SmiConstant(2)

   When `outer` subsequently loads `x` from `A`, it folds to the stale
   `1` instead of `2`.

A similar bug occurs if `B'` folds to `B = HeapConstant(ctx_mid)` while
`A = HeapConstant(ctx_outer)` is also in the graph: walking `A'` to `B`
classifies both `A'` and `A` as `kOnlyLoadsRelativeToConstant`, which
merges to `kOnlyLoadsRelativeToConstant` rather than `kAlways`.

Move `Opcode::kLoadContextSlotNoCells` into the switch in
KnownNodeAspects::UpdateMayHaveAliasingContexts and only treat
`PREVIOUS_INDEX` chains as `kOnlyLoadsRelativeToCurrentContext` when
their root is `InitialValue`, setting `kAlways` for any other root.

